server.ts:1456-1459: `// Structured audit recorder. Tests pass a no-op sink; production wires MemoryStore.
const auditSink: AuditSink = { insert: () => { /* no-op default */ } };
terminalAudit = new TerminalAudit(auditSink);` — the comment claims "production wires MemoryStore" but the actual code creates an `AuditSink` whose `insert()` is a no-op lambda. No `integrity` config is passed, so the HMAC chain branch in audit.ts:74 (`if (this.integrity && isChainedSink(this.store))`) is never entered. The entire audit-integrity.ts chain mechanism (computeAuditHmac, loadOrCreateAuditKey, verifyAuditChain) exists but is never activated at runtime — only the CLI command `audit-verify` calls it.

status-reconcile.ts:128-130: `// No dashboard data despite an apparent active sprint — return live empty.
if (!dashData || typeof dashData !== 'object') {
  return idleResponse(state.sprintId);
}` — the comment says "return live empty" but the code calls `idleResponse()` which sets `phase: 'IDLE', status: 'IDLE', idle: true, agents: [], progress: {done:0,...}`. This is the same idle shape as a completed sprint. The sprint-state.json can be ACTIVE (written by writeSprintState in sprint-phases.ts:425) before the Auditor has written the first `.dashboard` scan (typically seconds later).

Lines 196–202:
```typescript
if (tasks === 0) {
  // Fallback: count agent name mentions as a proxy
  tasks = mentions;
  success = mentions;
}
```
When the sprint log file contains no markdown table rows (`taskLineRegex` finds nothing), the code falls back to setting both `tasks` and `success` equal to the raw mention count — yielding 100% success rate. A sprint file that logs an agent failure via prose (e.g. error messages, NO_GO notes) would mention the agent name and still produce a synthetic 100% success record.

Lines 43–47 (vi.mock block):
```typescript
vi.mock('../../src/core/memory-store.js', () => ({
  MemoryStore: mockMemoryStoreCtor,
}));
```
The entire `MemoryStore` class is replaced with a mock. `appendChatTurn` and `getChatHistory` are `vi.fn()` stubs. No SQL is executed. The tests assert that `mockMemoryStoreCtor` was called and that `vi.fn()` was called with correct args — they never verify that a real SQLite row is written or read. Combined with finding #1 (buildChatMemoryAdapter has no production callers), these tests exercise neither the real persistence path nor the live REPL wire.

Lines 168–172:
```ts
await runSprint(projectRoot, config, {
  autoApprove: opts.autoApprove,
});
```
The `allCompletedIds` set (line 76–79) that correctly identifies tasks completed before or during the crash is computed locally but never forwarded to `runSprint`. `RunSprintOptions` (sprint-controller.ts:595–623) has no `completedTaskIds` / `skipTaskIds` parameter. `runSprint` will therefore re-dispatch all tasks it finds in `.tasks/*.json` that are still in PENDING status, which includes tasks whose results were already written to disk before the crash.

